fic polynomial (0x04C11DB7) and incorporates the file length into the checksum computation\n   - For R1.1-R1.4 only, the implementation needs: CRC-32 computation, file reading, stdin support, multi-file processing, and error handling with continue-on-error\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - Message 1: The massive task specification including role, repository_files, project_context (vision, architecture, specifications, roadmap, analysis, requirement_states), execution_constitution, go_style_constitution, task description, constraints, shared_protocols, and package_contracts. The core request is to implement cmd/cksum for srd077 R1.1-R1.4.\n   - Message 2: \"CRITICAL: Respond with TEXT ONLY. Do NOT call any tools.\" - requesting a summary of the conversation.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Create `cmd/cksum/main.go` implementing POSIX CRC-32 checksum (R1.1-R1.4)\n   - Create `cmd/cksum/cksum_test.go` with differential tests against gcksum\n   - Run `go vet ./cmd/cksum/...` to verify no warnings\n   - Run `go build -o bin/cksum ./cmd/cksum/` to verify compilation\n   - Run `go test ./cmd/cksum/... -count=1` to verify tests pass\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I was in the initial research phase - I had just finished reading the four required reference files (SRD, md5sum, sha256sum, hashutil) and was about to begin implementing the code. No code has been written yet. The next step was to write both `cmd/cksum/main.go` and `cmd/cksum/cksum_test.go` based on the patterns observed in the reference implementations, adapted for CRC-32's different output format.\n\n   Key implementation details gathered:\n   - POSIX CRC-32 uses polynomial 0x04C11DB7 with bit-reversal (same as `hash/crc32.IEEE` table but with length appended)\n   - Actually, POSIX cksum uses the POSIX CRC algorithm which processes bytes through a lookup table, then folds in the file length byte-by-byte, then complements the result\n   - Output format: `%u %lld %s\\n` (decimal CRC, decimal byte count, filename) or `%u %lld\\n