{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KtKKqqxpkkUZTMvpYqjGvJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"GNU `cksum` treats `-` as a filename that gets printed in the output, not as stdin shorthand. When passed `-` as an explicit argument, gcksum prints `1219131554 3 -` (with the `-` filename). My implementation treats `-` as stdin and omits the filename.\n\nWait, looking at the output: `ref stdout: \"1219131554 3 -\\n\"` — so GNU cksum DOES print `-` as the filename when `-` is explicitly passed. But it still reads from stdin (the CRC matches).\n\nSo the behavior is: `-` means \"read from stdin\" but the filename `-` is still printed in the output. \n\nThe filename is only omitted when there are no arguments at all. I need to fix this by always printing the filename in the output, except when no arguments are provided. The rule should be: no arguments reads from stdin and outputs just `CRC SIZE`, but `-` as an explicit argument reads from stdin and outputs `CRC SIZE -`, and a regular filename outputs `CRC SIZE filename`. I'll restructure the code so the filename printing logic is consistent.\n\nRefactoring the approach...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":692,"cache_read_input_tokens":45926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":692},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52d80aaf-ef06-4287-b4c1-00f07d76a028","uuid":"c2ccce3b-961c-4178-8bf6-da56df81b91d"}
